body:
I can only comment about the Jolt integration part and that looks good to me. 

If I can add some minor things (which I think make the code nicer, but I'm fine with the current approach as well):

* You could remove some of the code by moving:

```
void JoltHeightMapShape3D::_update_material(JPH::RefConst<JoltPhysicsMaterial> &p_material) {
	if (!jolt_ref) {
		return;
	}
	jolt_ref_mutex.lock();
	JPH::PhysicsMaterialRefC array[] = { static_cast<JPH::PhysicsMaterialRefC>(p_material) };
	jolt_ref->RestoreMaterialState(array, 1);
	jolt_ref_mutex.unlock();
}
```
to `JoltShape3D` as every shape has a `RestoreMaterialState` call. This allows removing the function from all derived classes. 

* `_get_material` always allocates a new material and if I understand the code correctly, this now gets called unconditionally for every shape on shape construction. Essentially this duplicates the `friction` and `bounce` variables from `JoltShape3D` so you could also opt to store those values only in `JoltPhysicsMaterial` and add a `Ref<JoltPhysicsMaterial>` to `JoltShape3D`. This means you can remove `_update_material` completely as the material will be set on shape construction and when `friction` and `bounce` need to be updated you just update `friction` and `bounce` inside `JoltPhysicsMaterial`. This also allows removing some ifs in `JoltContactListener3D::_override_contact_properties` as nothing is using `JPH::PhysicsMaterial::sDefault`. B.t.w. the concept behind `JPH::PhysicsMaterial::sDefault` is that you can replace it with your own default material, so it is also possible to assign a `JoltPhysicsMaterial` to it with NaN `friction` and `bounce`.

* Having a single material on mesh/height field shapes is quite restrictive in my experience. We allow painting physics materials per triangle (with a max of X materials per mesh). This is maybe something that could be added in the future.

> Does this have any chance of working with Heightmap Shapes? [...] In Terrain3D I would like to be able to send a physics material "ID" for each Quad.

@Xtarsia Not as-is, no. The same goes for individual triangles in `ConcavePolygonShape3D`. It's technically doable with Jolt, which supports this for both shapes, but it's not immediately clear to me how you'd go about implementing it for Godot Physics.

Either way, we'd need some way of actually assigning material values to groups of faces, both from a UX perspective and from an API perspective, which is likely quite a bit of work, and better suited for a future PR. The texture approach might make some sense for `HeightMapShape3D`, but I'm not sure it suits `ConcavePolygonShape3D` as well.
